Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• ORDERS."order_id": unique identifier of each order – needed to count distinct orders.  
• ORDERS."status": overall order life‑cycle state – filter to keep only completed orders.  
• ORDERS."created_at": micro‑second epoch timestamp – derive the calendar month of the order.  

• ORDER_ITEMS."order_id": lets us link an order line back to the parent order.  
• ORDER_ITEMS."product_id": connects an order line to its product (and therefore category).  
• ORDER_ITEMS."sale_price": revenue of each order line – aggregate to monthly revenue.  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status": extra guard (many records are already “Complete”).  
• ORDER_ITEMS."created_at": alternative time‑stamp in case line‑item grain is required.  

• PRODUCTS."id": primary key of each product – used to join with ORDER_ITEMS."product_id".  
• PRODUCTS."category": product category we must group by.  

Typical conditions  
• ORDERS."status" = 'Complete' (core filter).  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status" = 'Complete'  (optional extra safeguard).  
• Ignore cancelled / returned lines by checking “returned_at” IS NULL if necessary.  

Below are ten Snowflake SQL snippets (LIMIT 20) that progress from simple exploration to the final pieces needed for the requested analysis.  
Remember to replace /*YOUR_CATEGORY*/ with the actual category name identified in step 9.

